From 5555f34e10c9fbc9ef855b9aa36876bf7442f9d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:58:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVDDemuxFFmpeg, DVD: a film in the fields of interlaced video
 is told before playback

The display mode of a video is chosen once, before its first picture, and never again within it.
For a film in the fields of interlaced video (FieldCadence.h) that means telling it before
playback, which needs its pictures decoded: the flags of an MPEG-2 stream do not say it.

- A file (where its soft telecine is told, PrefetchForSoftTelecine()): the pictures of its start,
  as the demuxer reads them ahead, are decoded too (72 at the most, within the same 1.5 s and
  4 MiB), and 2 MiB of the file at 50, 10 and 75 % of its length, read with a handle of their own
  (its video taken out of a program stream, a transport stream or the blocks of a Matroska file),
  in 1.5 s at the most and not over the internet. Only an MPEG-2 stream at 25, 29.97 or 30 frames
  a second that is no soft telecine.
- A DVD image or folder: the scan of its titles that tells soft telecine (CDiscSoftTelecineScan,
  on its own navigator and thread) decodes 2 MiB of video at the start of each title and at the
  same three places, and the navigator gives it with the title when it starts.

Where every place that told says the same film and none says video, the stream says the rate of
the film (23.976, or 24, for 3:2; 25000/1001 for 25 in 30; its own for 2:2) and the cadence, and
Kodi's own choice of a refresh rate picks the mode for it. Anything else plays as before, and so
does every stream with the setting off (the decoder of the probe is not even made then).

Measured on the host on the synthetic corpus (128 clips and containers: VOB, M2V, MKV, TS): every
film clip is told its cadence, in every container; no clip of video, and no clip that mixes film
and video, is told film; a cartoon on twos one field off or at 25 in 30, and still pictures, are
told nothing and play as before.
